/**
   @page recon_main The Conversation Manager library (recon)

   recon is a SIP user agent library with media support, built on the
   @ref dum_main "Dialog Usage Manager". Applications work with
   conversations (audio mixes) and the participants in them, and recon takes
   care of the SIP signalling, SDP offer/answer, NAT traversal and media
   for them. The MOHParkServer (resip/recon/MOHParkServer) and reConServer
   (apps/reConServer) applications are built on it.

   @section recon_classes Main Classes
      An application subclasses two classes:
      - recon::UserAgent : Startup, the process loop and shutdown; user agent
      settings (recon::UserAgentMasterProfile); application timers;
      conversation profiles and registrations; and subscriptions.
      - recon::ConversationManager : Creating and destroying conversations
      and participants, placing and answering calls, playing tones and
      files, and recording. The application implements its on...()
      callbacks, such as onIncomingParticipant(), onParticipantAlerting(),
      onParticipantConnected() and onParticipantTerminated().

      recon::ConversationProfile extends resip::UserProfile with per-identity
      settings, such as the SDP to offer, the NAT traversal mode (STUN, or a
      TURN allocation over UDP, TCP or TLS) and the secure media mode (SRTP
      keyed through SDP, or DTLS-SRTP).

   @section recon_model Conversations and Participants
      A conversation is a set of participants whose audio is mixed together.
      A participant can be in more than one conversation (with some limits,
      depending on the media stack). Participants are:
      - remote participants : SIP calls, created with
      recon::ConversationManager::createRemoteParticipant() or reported
      through onIncomingParticipant();
      - the local participant : the local microphone and speaker, created with
      recon::ConversationManager::createLocalParticipant();
      - media resource participants : tones, files and recordings, created with
      recon::ConversationManager::createMediaResourceParticipant() from URLs
      such as tone:ringback, file:prompt.wav;repeat, cache:welcome and
      record:call.wav;
      - IM participants : instant messaging, either in pager mode (standalone
      SIP MESSAGE requests, createRemoteIMPagerParticipant()) or in session
      mode (MESSAGE requests inside an INVITE dialog that offers a
      Microsoft-style "message" media line,
      createRemoteIMSessionParticipant()). Messages are sent with
      sendIMToParticipant().

      Participants are moved between conversations with addParticipant(),
      removeParticipant() and moveParticipant(), and
      modifyParticipantContribution() sets a participant's input and output
      gain in each conversation.

   @section recon_media Media Stacks
      Media is handled by a recon::MediaStackAdapter, set on the
      ConversationManager. recon::SipXMediaStackAdapter uses sipXtapi
      (CMake option USE_SIPXTAPI), and there are adapters for GStreamer
      (USE_GSTREAMER), Kurento (USE_KURENTO) and LibWebRTC (USE_LIBWEBRTC).
      With sipXtapi, @ref reflow_main "reflow" carries the RTP, and provides
      STUN, TURN, SRTP and DTLS-SRTP.

   @section recon_usage Usage
      - Subclass recon::ConversationManager and recon::UserAgent.
      - Create the ConversationManager, then its media stack adapter (which
      takes the ConversationManager), and set the adapter with
      setMediaStackAdapter().
      - Create a recon::UserAgentMasterProfile, and a recon::UserAgent with it
      and the ConversationManager.
      - Add at least one recon::ConversationProfile with
      recon::UserAgent::addConversationProfile(), with one set as the
      default for outgoing calls.
      - Call recon::UserAgent::startup(), then recon::UserAgent::process() in
      a loop, and recon::UserAgent::shutdown() when done.

      resip/recon/test/testUA.cxx is a complete example.

   @section recon_threads Threads
      The ConversationManager's on...() callbacks are called from the thread
      that runs recon::UserAgent::process(). Most ConversationManager
      functions, such as createConversation() and addParticipant(), do not
      act immediately: they return a handle at once, post a command to the
      Dialog Usage Manager's queue and do the work when that thread next
      runs.
*/
